---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const life = [
  ['11 May 1912', 'Born at Berlin', 'To Auguste Luwinski, nineteen and unmarried. He took her surname and kept it for life.', 'doc'],
  ['5 Jan 1924', 'His parents marry', 'Auguste Luwinski and Jacob Leibholz, at Berlin-Schöneberg — twelve years after his birth.', 'doc'],
  ['5 Aug 1925', 'A brother', 'Siegfried Leibholz, born at Martin-Luther-Straße 60, Berlin-Schöneberg. Same parents; the other surname.', 'doc'],
  ['1933', 'Emigrates to Johannesburg', 'Aged twenty-one, in the year the Nazis took power.', 'doc'],
  ['from 1933', 'South Africa', 'Occupation recorded as dress designer — his mother was a Schneiderin und Modistin.', 'doc'],
  ['26 Feb 1943', 'His father deported', 'Jacob Leibholz, from Berlin to Auschwitz. Kurt had been ten years gone.', 'doc'],
  ['19 Jun 1948', 'Marries Ivone', 'At Johannesburg, certificate 858. He is 36, she is 24 and entered as Ivone de Figueiredo de Witt.', 'doc'],
  ['after Jun 1948', 'Files for divorce', 'Within weeks of the wedding, and before his son was born.', 'doc'],
  ['24 Apr 1949', 'Birth of Derrick', '', 'doc'],
  ['1951', 'Divorce', 'Illiquid case, restitution of conjugal rights: Kurt Luwinski versus Ivone, formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo.', 'doc'],
  ['29 Feb 1956', 'Marries Frances Hostowsky', 'Registered in the Hanover, Orange Free State records.', 'doc'],
  ['1957', 'Files for divorce again', 'Kurt Luwinski versus Frances, formerly Sefton, formerly Berner, born Hostowsky.', 'doc'],
  ['15 Dec 1968', 'Dies at Johannesburg', 'Aged 56. Buried the next day: Westpark, block L, grave 211. Estate file 7584/1969 follows in the new year.', 'doc'],
  ['16 Dec 1968', 'Buried Westpark, Johannesburg', 'By the Chevrah Kadisha. Yahrzeit 24 Kislev 5729. He was buried as a Jew.', 'doc'],
];
---
<Base title="Kurt Israel Luwinski"
  subtitle="Berlin, 11 May 1912 — Johannesburg, 15 December 1968"
  lede="He was born with his mother's name because his parents would not marry for another twelve years. He left Germany at twenty-one and never lived there again. His father, his uncles and his cousins were killed while he was cutting dresses eight thousand kilometres away.">

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th></th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {life.map(([when, what, detail, tag]) => (
        <tr><td>{when}</td><td><strong>{what}</strong></td><td>{detail}</td><td><span class={'tag ' + tag}>Documented</span></td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>1933</h2>
  <p>
    The tree records his emigration in a single word and a year: <em>Johannesburg, Süd Afrika, 1933</em>.
    He was twenty-one. His father was a Kaufmann in Schöneberg; his mother a dressmaker from an East
    Prussian farming family. He arrived in a city with an established German-Jewish trade and took up
    a needle trade of his own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ten years later, on 26 February 1943, his father was put on a train from Berlin to Auschwitz — the
    day before the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>, the round-up of the last Jewish forced labourers in the city.
    Within six weeks his uncle Samuel was dead at Auschwitz too. Nothing in the record says what Kurt
    knew, or when.
  </p>

  <h2>The stone</h2>
  <p>
    Westpark Cemetery, block L, grave 211. Photographed by the burial society, and it carries more
    than his dates.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    אִישׁ יָקָר וְחָבִיב<br />
    <strong>ר׳ לייב קלמן בר דוד</strong><br />
    נפטר כ״ד כסלו תשכ״ט<br />
    תנצב״ה
    <br /><br />
    IN CHERISHED MEMORY OF MY DEAR HUSBAND<br />
    <strong>(LU) KURT LUWINSKI</strong><br />
    WHO PASSED AWAY <strong>15TH DECEMBER 1968</strong>, <strong>AGE 56</strong><br />
    ALWAYS REMEMBERED BY HIS LOVING <strong>WIFE PAULA</strong>, <strong>SON</strong>,
    <strong>MOTHER</strong> AND FRIENDS
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <em>“A dear and beloved man, R' Leib Kalman bar David, died 24 Kislev 5729.”</em> Pauline — Paula
    — put it up. <strong>His mother was alive</strong> and is named on it; Auguste outlived him by
    seven years. <strong>“Son”</strong> is singular.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And his father's name is not Jacob <span class="tag doc">Documented reading</span></h3>
    <p>
      A <em>matzevah</em> names the father. Jacob Leibholz would ordinarily be
      <strong>יעקב — Yaakov</strong>. The stone says <strong>בר דוד</strong>: <strong>bar David</strong>,
      son of David.
    </p>
    <p>Three explanations fit, and one photograph will not choose between them:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Jacob's Hebrew name was David.</strong> German Jews often carried a civil name and a Hebrew name that were not translations of each other. Ordinary, though not the default.</li>
      <li><strong>Kurt's father was a man called David</strong> — and Jacob, who married Auguste twelve years after Kurt was born and is named as his father only in the family record, was not.</li>
      <li><strong>Paula supplied the name</strong>, in Johannesburg in 1968, for a father-in-law she never met who had been dead a quarter of a century in a country she had probably never seen.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small">
      The second would be a second paternity break, one generation above the one this archive
      already found. The third is the dullest and, on this evidence, no less likely.
      <span class="tag inf">Open</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A further oddity</h3>
    <p>
      His mother was a Catholic East Prussian, so by halakha Kurt was <strong>not Jewish</strong> —
      and he was nonetheless buried by the Chevrah Kadisha, under <em>Reb</em>, with a Hebrew name in
      the standard <em>ben</em> form. A convert is normally written <em>ben Avraham</em>; he is not.
      Either the community did not apply the test, or something about his mother is not what the
      family record says.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The name</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Where does “Israel” come from? <span class="tag inf">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      From 1 January 1939 the Nazi state compelled German Jewish men whose first names were not on an
      approved list to insert <em>Israel</em>, and women <em>Sara</em>. It is why the tree also carries
      a “Samuel <em>Israel</em> Leibholz”.
    </p>
    <p>
      But Kurt had been in South Africa for six years by then; and as the son of a Jewish father and a
      Catholic East Prussian mother he would ordinarily have been classed <em>Mischling ersten Grades</em>,
      to whom the name decree did not apply. The Federal Archives' own entry for his uncle reads plainly
      “Samuel”, not “Samuel Israel” — so the <em>Israel</em> on both men in this family's tree came from
      some other document. Which document is not yet known. It is worth finding: it would say how the
      German state classified him, and whether the classification followed him out of the country.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Three wives, two divorces filed</h2>
  <p>
    Ivone in 1948, divorced 1951. Frances Hostowsky in 1956, divorce filed 1957. Pauline — recorded in
    the tree under the names Rappoport and Miller, born 29 September 1914, died of pancreatic cancer on
    17 November 1985 — appears as his wife without a date, and outlived him by sixteen years. Both
    divorce actions are “illiquid case, restitution of conjugal rights”, which in South African practice
    of the period was the standard first step towards a decree, not a reconciliation.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>He died in 1968, not 1969 <span class="tag doc">Corrected</span></h3>
    <p>
      The family tree gives 15 December <strong>1969</strong>, and this archive repeated it. The
      Johannesburg Chevrah Kadisha burial register gives <strong>15 December 1968</strong> — and the
      Hebrew date settles it independently: <strong>24 Kislev 5729</strong> is 15 December 1968. Two
      datings of the same day, agreeing with each other and not with the tree.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also makes the estate file read naturally instead of oddly. <strong>MHG 7584/69</strong> is
      an estate opened in 1969 on a death the previous December, which is ordinary; an estate opened
      in the same month would have been the strange thing.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>So Kurt was 56 when he died, not 57 — and his son was nineteen, not twenty.</strong>
      He lies at Westpark, <strong>block L, grave 211</strong>. Pauline was buried twenty-six years
      later in block V, grave 1470, still under his name. There is no family plot.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The national archives settle the end of it. His estate file — <strong>Transvaal, Master of the High
    Court, 7584/69, “Luwinski, Kurt”</strong> — carries the remark <em>“surviving spouse Pauline
    Luwinski”</em>. So Pauline was his widow, and the undated marriage in the family tree was his last.
    <span class="tag doc">Documented</span> The same index shows Frances remarrying in 1958, the year
    after he sued her: <strong>Rolf Eisenhardt and Frances Luwinski</strong>, formerly Sefton, formerly
    Berner, born Hostowski, in the Cape Town and Wynberg marriage register.
  </p>

  <h2>His brother, who stayed</h2>
  <p>
    Siegfried Leibholz was born at Martin-Luther-Straße 60 in Schöneberg on 5 August 1925 — the same
    parents, the father's surname. The residence record is the part that stops you: from
    <strong>January 1943 to 30 April 1945</strong> he was at Sommerfeld in the Osthavelland, where his
    mother's people farmed. His father was deported from Berlin on 26 February 1943. The boy was moved
    out of the city in the weeks before it happened, and stayed out until the war ended.
  </p>
  <p>
    He joined the Berlin Kreispolizei on 15 February 1947, married Edith Charlotte Zelenka, had a son
    Michael Kurt in 1951 — named, at least in part, for the brother in Johannesburg — and died at
    Potsdam on 1 February 2005. He is buried at the Südwestkirchhof, Stahnsdorf.
  </p>
</Base>
